course: world
lesson: 42
title: Continents and oceans
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Most of the Earth's surface is covered by water: about seven tenths is ocean and only about three tenths is land.
explain: The great pieces of land are called continents. There are seven: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe and Australia.
explain: The great bodies of salt water are the oceans: the Pacific, the Atlantic, the Indian, the Southern and the Arctic. In fact they all join into one world ocean.
explain: Asia is the largest continent and Australia the smallest; the Pacific is the largest and deepest ocean, so big that all the land on Earth would fit into it.
explain: The United States and Canada are in North America, the United Kingdom and Ireland in Europe, and Australia is a continent all by itself; New Zealand lies in the Pacific south-east of Australia. Europe and Asia are joined by land, and the Ural Mountains are usually taken as the border between them.
fact: About seven tenths of the Earth's surface is covered by oceans.
fact: There are seven continents: Asia, Africa, North America, South America, Antarctica, Europe and Australia.
fact: Asia is the largest continent and has the most people.
fact: Australia is the smallest continent.
fact: Antarctica lies around the South Pole, is covered by thick ice and has no towns where people live all year.
fact: There are five oceans: the Pacific, the Atlantic, the Indian, the Southern and the Arctic.
fact: The Pacific Ocean is the largest and the deepest ocean.
fact: The Arctic Ocean is the smallest ocean and lies around the North Pole.
fact: All the oceans are joined together, so a ship can sail from one to another.
fact: The United States and Canada are in North America, and the United Kingdom is in Europe.
fact: The Atlantic Ocean lies between Europe and Africa on one side and North and South America on the other.
fact: France, with its capital Paris, is in Europe; China is in Asia.
fact: Europe and Asia are joined by land; the Ural Mountains are often taken as the border between them.
exercise: What covers most of the Earth's surface? | Ocean water | Dry land | Ice and snow | Forests
exercise: How many continents are there? | Seven | Five | Six | Nine
exercise: Which is the largest continent? | Asia | Africa | Europe | North America
exercise: Which is the smallest continent? | Australia | Europe | Antarctica | South America
exercise: Which is the largest and deepest ocean? | The Pacific Ocean | The Atlantic Ocean | The Indian Ocean | The Arctic Ocean
exercise: Which ocean lies around the North Pole? | The Arctic Ocean | The Southern Ocean | The Indian Ocean | The Pacific Ocean
exercise: Which continent is covered by thick ice around the South Pole? | Antarctica | Australia | South America | Africa
exercise: On which continent are the United States and Canada? | North America | South America | Europe | Asia
exercise: What is a continent? | One of Earth's great areas of land | A large lake in the middle of a country | Any island with more than one town on it | A group of countries with one language
exercise: Which mountains are often taken as the border between Europe and Asia? | The Ural Mountains | The Rocky Mountains | The Alps | The Andes
exercise: Which continent has the most people living on it? | Asia | Europe | Australia | South America
exercise: How many oceans are usually counted? | Five | Three | Seven | Ten
exercise: Which ocean lies between Europe and Africa on one side and the Americas on the other? | The Atlantic Ocean | The Pacific Ocean | The Indian Ocean | The Arctic Ocean
exercise: Why can a ship sail from the Atlantic Ocean into the Indian Ocean? | All the oceans are joined into one world ocean | Ships are carried across the land on trucks | There is a long canal through the middle of Africa | The two oceans are really the same ocean with two names
test: If you could look at the Earth from space, what would you see most of? | Blue ocean | Green forests | Yellow deserts | White ice
test: A scientist works for a few months a year on a continent with no towns, covered with thick ice. Where is she? | Antarctica | Australia | Asia | Europe
test: Which list contains only continents? | Africa, Europe, Australia | Africa, Pacific, Europe | Asia, Arctic, Canada | Atlantic, Indian, Europe
test: Which list contains only oceans? | Pacific, Atlantic, Indian | Pacific, Asia, Arctic | Atlantic, Black, Europe | Indian, Africa, Southern
test: Which is true? | Asia is the largest continent and Australia the smallest | Africa is the largest continent and Europe the smallest | Europe is the largest continent and Asia the smallest | All seven continents are about the same size
test: A traveller drives from Paris to China without crossing any sea. How is that possible? | Europe and Asia are joined by land | A long bridge crosses the Pacific Ocean | China is also in Europe | There is a tunnel under the Atlantic Ocean
test: Which statement about the Pacific Ocean is true? | All the land on Earth would fit inside it | It is the smallest and shallowest of the five oceans | It lies all around the North Pole under the ice | It is a huge lake of fresh water you can drink
test: Why is the Earth sometimes called the blue planet? | Because most of its surface is covered by water | Because its land is mostly blue rock | Because its sky is blue only from space | Because it is closer to the Sun than other planets
test: What is the ocean around the North Pole called? | The Arctic Ocean | The Southern Ocean | The Indian Ocean | The Atlantic Ocean
test: biggest ocean, deepest too, which one | The Pacific | The Atlantic | The Indian | The Arctic
test: can u actually sail a boat from the atlantic into the pacific | Yes, the oceans are all joined | No, the oceans are separate seas | Only through a river across the land | Only in the frozen winter, over the ice
test: my classmate says the Arctic is a continent with land at the North Pole. true? | No, the Arctic is an ocean; Antarctica is the icy continent | Yes, the North Pole is on a continent | Yes, it is the eighth continent | No, the Arctic is part of Europe
test: Noah reckons Australia's the biggest continent. right? | No, Australia is the smallest and Asia is the largest | Yes, Australia is the largest of all | Yes, it is bigger than Asia | No, Africa is the smallest and Australia is the largest
exam: State the number of continents. | Seven | Five | Six | Eight
exam: Identify the largest of the continents. | Asia | Africa | North America | Antarctica
exam: Identify the largest and deepest of the oceans. | The Pacific Ocean | The Atlantic Ocean | The Indian Ocean | The Arctic Ocean
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the boundary between Europe and Asia. | The Ural Mountains are often taken as the border | The two are separated entirely by an ocean | The River Thames is taken as the border | The Alps are taken as the border
